[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 15:43] Hold that. Beyond a price increase, what other levers could the client pull to reverse this? Give me a structured set.

[candidate · 17:13] Sure — I'd group levers into four buckets.

First, pricing. Beyond a blanket increase: targeted increases on the most inelastic items, sizing changes, and a premium tier. Specialty coffee tends to have loyal, less price-sensitive customers, which makes this the most direct lever.

Second, cost of goods. Renegotiate supplier contracts, lock in forward contracts or hedge on beans to stop the bleeding, consolidate vendors across 200 locations for scale, and reduce waste and spoilage. Also value-engineer the inputs — cup and packaging specs, dairy sourcing.

Third, mix. Push customers toward higher-margin items — black coffee is less dairy-intensive than a latte, and food or merchandise may carry different margins. Bundling can raise ticket size without a visible price hike.

Fourth, the cost lines we set aside. Overhead crept from 12 to 14 points; that's 2 points of margin and worth a look, even if it's secondary.

I'd sequence pricing and procurement first — they're the fastest and match the root cause.
